   "body_html": "Because the book is old."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 53151039,
   "parent_id": 32652213,
   "parent_type": "question",
   "author_user_id": 28169,
   "author_display_name": "unwind",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": null,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/questions/32652213/#comment53151039_32652213",
   "score": 13,
   "created_utc": "2015-09-18T12:38:38Z",
   "edited": false,
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "Because even the Sun has its dark spots, would be my answer. In other words, the book is wrong. It could be that the text predates the semantics for <code>void *</code>, and wasn&#39;t updated. See also <a href=\"http://stackoverflow.com/a/28460192/28169\">this answer</a>."
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 32652435,
   "parent_id": 32652213,
   "author_user_id": 1606345,
   "author_display_name": "David Ranieri",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/32652435",
   "score": 231,
   "created_utc": "2015-09-18T12:47:40Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2015-09-18T13:10:10Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2015-09-18T13:10:10Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>From <a href=\"http://computer-programming-forum.com/47-c-language/a9c4a586c7dcd3fe.htm\">http://computer-programming-forum.com/47-c-language/a9c4a586c7dcd3fe.htm</a>:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>In pre-ANSI C -- as described in K&amp;R-1 -- malloc() returned a <code>char *</code>\n  and it was necessary to cast its return value in all cases where the\n  receiving variable was not also a <code>char *</code>.  The new <code>void *</code> type in\n  Standard C makes these contortions unnecessary.</p>\n  \n  <p>To save anybody from the embarrassment of leaping needlessly to the\n  defence of K&amp;R-2, I asked Dennis Ritchie for an opinion that I could\n  quote on the validity of the sentence cited above from page 142.  He\n  replied:</p>\n  \n  <blockquote>\n    <p><em>In any case, now that I reread the stuff on p. 142, I think it's\n        wrong; it's written in such a way that it's not just defensive\n        against earlier rules, it misrepresents the ANSI rules.</em></p>\n  </blockquote>\n</blockquote>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 53151808,
   "parent_id": 32652435,
   "parent_type": "answer",
   "author_user_id": 4745612,
